  "upgrade_suggestion": "Your 1982 Specialty pressing is an excellent original cut. The ultimate audiophile vinyl upgrade is the 1983 Mobile Fidelity Sound Lab (MFSL 1-086) \"Original Master Recording,\" pressed on JVC Super Vinyl in Japan. For high-resolution digital playback on your Sony UBP-X700, look for the 2013 Audio Fidelity hybrid SACD (AFZ 164) mastered by Kevin Gray.",
  "commentary": "Released on August 3, 1982 by Warner Bros. Records, *If That's What It Takes* is the debut solo studio album by Michael McDonald. Following his highly successful tenure fronting the Doobie Brothers (which ended with the band's temporary dissolution that same year), the album solidified McDonald as a premier voice of the L.A. Pop and \"blue-eyed soul\" sound of the early 1980s. Co-produced by Ted Templeman and Lenny Waronker alongside McDonald, the record boasts an exceptionally polished production that has long made it a favorite among audiophiles. The lead single, \"I Keep Forgettin' (Every Time You're Near)\"—co-written with Ed Sanford—became a massive crossover hit, defined by Greg Phillinganes' clavinet/Rhodes groove, Louis Johnson's bassline, and a distinct drum performance by Jeff Porcaro.\n\nThe album features a legendary roster of L.A. session musicians, including Steve Gadd and Jeff Porcaro sharing drum duties, Willie Weeks and Louis Johnson on bass, Steve Lukather and Dean Parks on guitars, and Tom Scott contributing memorable saxophone work. Horn arrangements were handled by Jerry Hey, and Marty Paich provided the lush string arrangements. The album’s clean, dynamic, and meticulously layered tracks are widely considered a masterclass in early-80s West Coast pop studio craftsmanship.\n\n**About this 1982 Specialty pressing (LP, Stereo):** Warner Bros. Records catalog `9 23703-1` (sleeve/spine) and `1-23703` (labels), barcode `07599237031`. This pressing was manufactured by the Specialty Records Corporation (indicated by the `SP` suffix on the labels and the Specialty stampers). \n\nDeadwax runout details confirm the classic L.A. mastering and pressing chain for this copy. Side A carries the matrix `1-23703-A SR-3 SP 1-1`, indicating it was cut at Westlake with the `SR-3` mastering designation and pressed from the first set of stampers at Specialty. \n\nYour copy's Side B runout contains the matrix `1-23704-B S1 B-16965-S1 SP` alongside the Allied Record Company logo (`[Allied ɑ logo]`), `SLM` (Sheffield Lab Matrix), and the delta symbol `△` (which looks like a pyramid) followed by `2074-X`. This documents the shared manufacturing chain between the Allied plating facility, Sheffield metalwork, and the Specialty pressing plant common for major Warner Bros. releases of the era.\n",
